# grammar3.gr -- grammar2.gr plus the phenomena of Section 2.2.
#
# Conventions (inherited from grammar.gr):
#    - terminals are lowercase              (president)
#    - preterminals are capitalized         (Noun)
#    - other nonterminals are all-caps      (NP)
#
# Verbs are split into preterminals by SUBCATEGORIZATION -- that is, by which
# complements the verb demands.  That is what keeps the grammar from producing
# *"the president thought that a sandwich sighed a pickle .": "sighed" is a
# Vintrans, and no rule lets a Vintrans take an object.
#
#    Vintrans  no complement                sighed, worked
#    Vtrans    one object noun phrase       ate, kissed
#    Vcomp     a subordinate clause         thought, said
#    Vpsych    an object, and optionally    perplexed, surprised
#              a clause as its SUBJECT
#######################

# Rules for creating full sentences.

6	ROOT	S .
1	ROOT	S !
1	ROOT	is it true that S ?     # mixing terminals and nonterminals is ok.

# The basic grammar rules.  Here's what the abbreviations stand for:
#    S    = sentence
#    SBAR = sentence packaged up as a subordinate clause ("that ...")
#    NP   = noun phrase
#    VP   = verb phrase
#    PP   = prepositional phrase
#    Det  = determiner (sometimes called "article")
#    Prep = preposition
#    Adj  = adjective
#    Adv  = degree adverb modifying an adjective ("very")
#    Conj = coordinating conjunction

# CLAUSES.  Besides the ordinary subject-predicate clause, a subordinate
# clause can act as the subject of a psych verb (#6), and that clausal
# subject can be extraposed to the end, leaving a dummy "it" behind (#5).
24	S	NP VP
1	S	SBAR VPpsych
1	S	it VPpsych SBAR
1	S	S Conj S

1	SBAR	that S

# VERB PHRASES.  One rule per subcategorization frame, so a verb can only
# appear with the complements it actually allows.
8	VP	Vintrans
16	VP	Vtrans NP
3	VP	Vcomp SBAR
3	VP	VPpsych

# A psych verb is an ordinary transitive except that its subject may be a
# clause, so it gets its own phrase type for the two S rules above.
1	VPpsych	Vpsych NP

# Any verb phrase may be modified by a prepositional phrase.  This is what
# gives "the president worked on every proposal on the desk ." and, in the
# same stroke, "Sally ate a sandwich in the floor ."
3	VP	VP PP
1	VP	VP Conj VP

# NOUN PHRASES.
24	NP	Det Noun
5	NP	Proper
4	NP	NP PP
3	NP	NP Conj NP

5	Noun	Adj Noun

1	PP	Prep NP

# COORDINATION OF HEADS.  Coordinating two verbs that share one object is
# what licenses "wanted and ate a sandwich" (as opposed to the VP-level
# coordination above, which would give "wanted a sandwich and ate a pickle").
1	Vtrans	Vtrans Conj Vtrans
1	Vintrans	Vintrans Conj Vintrans

# Vocabulary.  Your program can see that "ate" is a terminal
# symbol because there exists no rule for rewriting it.
# Any symbol that can rewrite as a terminal (or a string of
# terminals, like "chief of staff") is called a "preterminal."  Notice
# that a preterminal is a special kind of nonterminal.

3	Vtrans	ate
3	Vtrans	wanted
2	Vtrans	kissed
2	Vtrans	understood
1	Vtrans	pickled

3	Vintrans	sighed
3	Vintrans	worked
1	Vintrans	waited

3	Vcomp	thought
2	Vcomp	said
2	Vcomp	knew
1	Vcomp	believed

# Any of these can replace "perplexed" in sentences #5 and #6.
3	Vpsych	perplexed
2	Vpsych	surprised
2	Vpsych	bothered
1	Vpsych	delighted

3	Det	the
3	Det	a
1	Det	every

2	Proper	Sally
1	Proper	Washington

2	Noun	president
3	Noun	sandwich
3	Noun	pickle
2	Noun	chief of staff
2	Noun	proposal
2	Noun	desk
1	Noun	floor

1	Adj	fine
2	Adj	delicious
2	Adj	perplexed
2	Adj	pickled

# Degree adverbs stack, which is what makes "very very very perplexed" work
# without listing each length separately.
1	Adj	Adv Adj
3	Adv	very
1	Adv	rather
1	Adv	quite

3	Conj	and
1	Conj	or

3	Prep	with
3	Prep	in
2	Prep	on
1	Prep	under
